  <p>
    If you have no reference image or want to design a palette from scratch, use the generator at <a href="https://kodinitools.com/kodini-color-extractor/generator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kodinitools.com/kodini-color-extractor/generator</a>. At the push of a button it creates harmonious color combinations based on color theory, for example monochromatic shades of a base tone. The spacebar delivers a new variant each time until one fits.
  </p>

  <h3>Features at a Glance</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Harmony:</strong> choose the rule by which the colors relate to each other, such as monochromatic.</li>
    <li><strong>Colors:</strong> set the number of fields in the palette.</li>
    <li><strong>Generate with the spacebar:</strong> flip through many suggestions quickly without touching the mouse.</li>
    <li><strong>Lock colors:</strong> if you like a tone, lock it and it survives the next generation.</li>
    <li><strong>Adjustments:</strong> change brightness, contrast, saturation and hue for all colors together or for a single field. Undo, redo and reset are available.</li>
    <li><strong>Pick color:</strong> click a field and fine-tune the tone directly in the color picker.</li>
    <li><strong>Copy:</strong> individual HEX values with one click, or the whole palette via "Copy all".</li>
    <li><strong>Export:</strong> the same nine formats as in the extractor — the generated palette goes straight into your project as CSS, SCSS, a Tailwind config or design tokens.</li>
  </ul>


  <div class="tip-box">
    <p><strong>Combine both parts:</strong> extract colors from a reference photo, take the main tone into the generator and create matching shades for buttons, backgrounds and text there. That turns one image into a complete color scheme.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Nine Export Formats in Both Tools</h2>

  <p>
    Colors are only useful once they reach your project. That is why the extractor and the palette generator offer the same nine export formats. Pick the one your tooling understands and download the finished file — no retyping, no reformatting.
  </p>

  <div class="blog-table-wrap">
    <table class="blog-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Format</th>
          <th>File</th>
          <th>Best for</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>HEX</td><td>.txt</td><td>The classic — a list for design tools, documentation, emails</td></tr>
        <tr><td>RGB</td><td>.txt</td><td>Image editing and programs that expect channel values</td></tr>
        <tr><td>RGBA</td><td>.txt</td><td>Like RGB, plus an alpha channel for transparency</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HSL</td><td>.txt</td><td>Hue, saturation, lightness — ideal for deriving shades</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HSLA</td><td>.txt</td><td>Like HSL, plus an alpha channel</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CSS custom properties</td><td>.css</td><td>Drop straight into any stylesheet as variables</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SCSS variables</td><td>.scss</td><td>For Sass projects with variables and mixins</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tailwind v3 config</td><td>.js</td><td>Move the colors into your <code>tailwind.config.js</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>W3C DTCG design tokens</td><td>.json</td><td>Standardized tokens for Figma, Style Dictionary and design systems</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The first five formats are plain text lists: one color per line, in exactly the notation you need. The other four are ready-made code files. The CSS file holds custom properties, the SCSS file named variables, the Tailwind file a color block for your configuration, and the JSON file design tokens following the standard of the W3C Design Tokens Community Group.
  </p>

  <div class="tip-box">
    <p><strong>Which format for what?</strong> For a classic website, CSS is enough. Working with Sass? Take SCSS. If your team uses Tailwind, the ready-made config saves you every bit of manual work. And when the colors need to hold across design and code alike, design tokens are the right choice.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Typical Use Cases</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Web design:</strong> read color codes from a logo or mood board and use them as CSS variables.</li>
    <li><strong>Presentations and social media:</strong> generate a palette that matches the cover image so graphics and photo work together.</li>
    <li><strong>Brand management:</strong> check which colors actually dominate in existing materials.</li>
    <li><strong>Interior design and crafts:</strong> determine tones from inspiration photos for wall paint or fabrics.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Privacy: Images Stay in Your Browser</h2>

  <p>
    Extraction, image editing and palette generation run entirely locally. No image is uploaded to a server. That makes the tool suitable for unpublished drafts and client material.
  </p>

